  "prompt_text": "The figure is a simple schematic comparing two ways to connect functional components, labeled A and B, using “click” strategies. A and B represent modular building blocks such as polymers, nanoparticles, surfaces, targeting units, reporter groups, drugs, or pharmacokinetic controllers.\n\nIn the upper scheme, covalent click chemistry links A and B through two complementary reactive partners, shown as blue and red boxes. After reaction, these partners join into a stable covalent bridge between the two components. This emphasizes a permanent, chemically bonded assembly.\n\nIn the lower scheme, non-covalent click chemistry uses a host–guest pair instead. One component carries a host-shaped binding site and the other carries a guest unit. These associate reversibly, as indicated by the two-way arrows, bringing A and B together without forming a covalent bond. The final complex is shown as the guest inserted into the host.\n\nOverall, the figure highlights the same modular design concept in two forms: covalent click chemistry for stable conjugation and non-covalent click chemistry for reversible self-assembly. The main message is that diverse functional entities can be combined either through permanent reactive coupling or through specific host–guest recognition.",
